From 7f52ec28f48df28afe4dc2acee9f1de3b5e3fe14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aria2-Pro-Core <builder@local>
Date: Sat, 23 May 2026 13:52:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] rpc: add removeFiles flag plus rpc-remove-files-default
 option

Adds an optional boolean parameter (index 1) to aria2.remove,
aria2.forceRemove and aria2.removeDownloadResult. When true, downloaded
files and their .aria2 control file are deleted along with the task or
result. When omitted, the new --rpc-remove-files-default option decides
(default false, matching upstream behavior). Explicit removeFiles=false
always keeps files, so existing clients are unaffected.

Active groups: the RPC handler stores the GID in RequestGroupMan and
the mark is consumed in ProcessStoppedRequestGroup after the I/O loop
releases file handles. When the mark is taken, the stop processing
skips saveControlFile and post-download handlers (no signature save,
no nextGroups insertion) and skips addDownloadResult so the task does
not bump numStoppedTotal_ or evict unrelated entries in
max-download-result. An explicit remove with file deletion overrides a
prior pause request.

Reserved/paused groups: the handler synchronously deletes files
before removeReservedGroup.

Stopped results: removeDownloadResult(gid, true) unlinks the per-entry
data file and a best-effort .aria2 control file.

Control file removal goes through group->removeControlFile() which
uses BtProgressInfoFile, so both the HTTP (<file>.aria2) and BT
(<basePath>/<name>.aria2) cases are handled. For multi-file torrents
the cleanup walks every file's ancestor chain up to the base path and
rmdir's deepest-first, leaving an empty top-level dir to be removed at
the end.
